.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.Dd September 7, 2026
.Dt BSDAUDIT 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm BSDAudit
.Nd authenticated BSM audit broker
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
provides the global
.Dq system.Audit
service from
.Pa /Capabilities/System/Audit.cap .
It runs as root because
.Xr audit 2
requires
.Dv PRIV_AUDIT_SUBMIT .
It cannot run in a jail because the kernel rejects audit submission from
jailed credentials.
Each client receives a capability-mode worker which prohibits fork, IPC,
incoming descriptor transfer, exec, and new sockets.
The supervisor retains each worker's process descriptor, observes exit through
.Dv EVFILT_PROCDESC ,
and admits at most 4096 concurrent workers.
On managed quiesce it first stops
admission, terminates and reaps every worker, and only then acknowledges
shutdown to
.Xr switchboard 8 .
.Pp
The switchboard-authenticated provider identity selects a fixed BSM event.
Requests cannot choose an event number, audit user ID, or provider identity.
Only the built-in CryptoCmp, NetworkCmp, LogCmp, and Notify identities are
accepted.
Each session is rate limited to a burst of 200 records and 100 records per
second.
.Sh SECURITY
.Nm
exists so functional components do not need root merely to produce trusted
audit records.
Subject and operation fields have fixed bounds and a restricted character
set.
Unknown providers are rejected before a worker is created.
The
.Xr audit 2
system call is capability-mode enabled but retains its root privilege check
and jail prohibition.
.Sh OBSERVABILITY
The
.Dq BSDAudit
DTrace provider exports
.Sy session ,
.Sy submit ,
and
.Sy reject
probes.
They report the authenticated provider label, selected fixed event, submitted
error value, and operation result without exposing an arbitrary audit record.
Submission and rejection outcomes are also represented by the broker's BSM
records where a record can be submitted safely.
.Sh SHUTDOWN
Startup and fatal event-loop errors release initialized resources.
Fatal event-loop errors also run worker shutdown.
Only a requested quiescence sends a completion acknowledgement, after worker
cleanup and event-queue closure.
Failed startup or event-loop exits return failure.
A failed worker-bootstrap handshake terminates and reaps the rejected child
before releasing its process descriptor.
The reported error remains the original handshake failure.
The supervisor allows a five-second graceful worker exit after SIGTERM.
If the monotonic clock cannot establish or check the deadline, it proceeds
to forced termination.
Interrupted final reap calls are retried.
A failed forced termination or final reap makes shutdown fail and suppresses
a successful quiescence acknowledgement.
Remaining reap attempts are nonblocking after a failure; the supervisor may
need to recover any surviving worker.
The grace period does not bound the final kernel reap wait.
Worker exit-event reaping retries interrupted calls and tolerates children
already reaped.
Other reap errors fail shutdown or the active service loop instead of being
silently discarded.
Parent bootstrap uses the shared library handshake with a single five-second
monotonic deadline for child status and acknowledgement.
Timeout follows the failed-worker cleanup path.
A child remains tracked after bootstrap or exit-watch setup failure.
These failures stop admission and enter controlled shutdown.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr audit 2 ,
.Xr libauditcmp 3 ,
.Xr libservice 3
